,0.0,-5.960462772236497e-07,-0.0030840712133795023,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.0002361257211305201,-9.536738616588991e-07,-0.0005185451591387391,-0.00016819016309455037,-3.576214658096433e-05,0.0,0.0,-0.00916559249162674,0.0,-4.768370445162873e-07,-0.07358275353908539,-1.2806304693222046,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.00037269797758199275,0.0,-5.149708886165172e-05,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.02086651138961315,-7.152555099310121e-07,0.0,0.0,-0.0008625839254818857,-5.578839045483619e-05,-2.3841830625315197e-06,-0.1534206122159958,-1.2993727978027891e-05,-7.152555099310121e-07],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,641,374,436,346,642,21495,400,25411,54876,9210,400,33,1706,3,323,9210,400,4897,32,3,6629,220,18,20,12348,13,3555,374,279,6629,315,9210,400,34,6352,3,30,508,6405,921,474,297,13842,62944,26,1159,17047,26,1379,7,16,17,20,1215,1638,2752,2333,258,28357,7,15,13,23,1106,7633,86154,1188,15,11,15,701,16,1106,16119,20912,37,3,44970,15,11,15,701,3741,4080,24,15,1106,12670,362,284,5419,7,16,21,15,701,425,284,5419,7,16,16,15,701,356,284,5419,7,19,15,701,422,284,5419,7,16,15,317,7633,4346,313,35,313,34,313,32,313,33,313,34,317,1502,20912,32,73315,32,49540,1215,2383,20912,33,73315,33,22037,1215,2383,20912,34,73315,34,42125,1215,2383,20912,35,73315,35,42125,317,24157,6405,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIn isosceles triangle $ABC$, angle $BAC$ and angle $BCA$ measure 35 degrees. What is the measure of angle $CDA$? [asy]\nimport olympiad; import geometry; size(125); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));\ndraw(circle((0,0),1));\ndot(\"$F$\",(0,0),dir(-90));\npair A = dir(160), B = dir(110), C = dir(40), D = dir(10);\ndraw(A--D--C--A--B--C);\nlabel(\"$A$\",A,W); label(\"$B$\",B,N); label(\"$C$\",C,E); label(\"$D$\",D,E);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"127b1ffef7a3bdec41382bbd4863df29f19b9f3d483fb27276d96a063b34fcc0"},"seconds":10.915424823760986,"seed":849941529744199271,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for the measure of angle \\( CDA \\) in the given isosceles triangle \\( ABC \\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the measure of angle \\( ABC \\):**\n   Since \\( \\triangle ABC \\) is isosceles with \\( \\angle BAC = \\angle BCA = 35^\\circ \\), we can find \\( \\angle ABC \\) by using the fact that the sum of the angles in a triangle is \\( 180^\\circ \\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAC - \\angle BCA = 180^\\circ - 35^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 110^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify the position and properties of point \\( D \\):**\n   The problem states that \\( D \\) is a point on the circumcircle of \\( \\triangle ABC \\) such that \\( AD \\) is a chord. Since \\( D \\) lies on the circumcircle, \\( \\angle BAC \\) and \\( \\angle BDC \\) are inscribed angles subtending the same arc \\( BC \\). Therefore, \\( \\angle BDC = \\angle BAC = 35^\\circ \\).\n\n3. **Determine the measure of angle \\( CDA \\):**\n   Since \\( \\angle BDC = 35^\\circ \\) and \\( \\angle BCA = 35^\\circ \\), we can find \\( \\angle CDA \\) by noting that \\( \\angle CDA \\) and \\( \\angle BDC \\) are supplementary angles (they form a straight line with \\( \\angle BCA \\) in the cyclic quadrilateral \\( ABDC \\)):\n   \\[\n   \\angle CDA = 180^\\circ - \\angle BDC = 180^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 145^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle \\( CDA \\) is \\(\\boxed{145}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{145}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"geometry:469"}
